---
/**
 * Where this shop stands on the way to being on the site — one screen, one answer.
 *
 * ── Why it replaced three cards, and then got a picture ──
 * The same errand was spread across a "not live yet" card on the overview, a clearing-status line, a
 * details form and a subscription card — four boxes, two tabs, and a seller left to work out which
 * one he was supposed to touch first (owner, 2026-08-24: *"יש פה גם סירבול של כפתורים ומידע למוכר.
 * צריך משהו מרוכז וברור"*). One screen fixed the scatter; it still made him READ three steps to
 * find out whether he was nearly there. So the screen now opens with the answer as a picture —
 * *"אני רוצה שיהיה ברור ליוזר באופן גרפי, איפה הוא עומד... אם האתר יכול או לא יכול להעלות לאוויר"*:
 * a state line, "step N of 3", and a three-segment bar. Everything under it is detail for the one
 * step that is his.
 *
 * **A segmented bar, not a percentage.** Three steps is three segments, and a segment maps to a row
 * below it — a continuous 66% bar would be inventing a precision the flow does not have (the middle
 * step is a wait of unknown length). The bar is in normal flow, so it is a border and a fill and no
 * shadow (Hard rules → elevation).
 *
 * ── The order changed on 2026-08-24, and the order is the point ──
 * It was details → PAY → wait for approval. So a seller paid, and then sat up to seven business days
 * with a shop that was not on the site — through exactly the week he is most likely to change his
 * mind in (owner: *"אני לא רוצה ליפול בין הכיסאות ושהמוכר יתחרט"*). Now paying is LAST: everything
 * before it is free, and the shop goes up in the same minute the card is charged.
 *
 * ── The middle step is nobody's to act on ──
 * PayMe examine every business and it takes up to seven business days (agreement §11). It is a step
 * because it is real time the seller will spend, and it is the one step with no control at all —
 * drawing a button there would invite him to do something that does not exist. Saying "we will
 * email you" is the whole of its job.
 *
 * ── The plan is chosen HERE, and that is the answer to "when does that screen even arrive" ──
 * `/pricing` is the public comparison, read before anybody has an account. The seller meets his own
 * plan at the moment it starts costing him, which is this step (owner, 2026-08-24). Four pills, the
 * current one marked, a link to the full comparison beside them — deliberately not a fifth decision
 * to agonise over: the difference between plans at a starting shop's revenue is a few shekels, it
 * can be changed in one click afterwards, and the whole goal is *"מינימום של התלבטות אם להירשם
 * למסלול או לא"*.
 *
 * ── Per SHOP, since the plan moved to the store ──
 * This screen is about the store the dashboard is currently on. Steps 1 and 2 are the seller's
 * ACCOUNT (one clearing account per registered business, however many shops); step 3 is this shop's
 * own plan and its own line on the monthly charge (`lib/store-plan.ts`).
 */
import type { PublishHold } from '../../lib/store-publication.js';
import type { MerchantKyc } from '../../lib/merchant-kyc.js';
import { SELLER_TIERS, type SellerTierId } from '../../lib/pricing.js';
import type { getT } from '../../i18n/index.js';
import ClearingDetailsForm from './ClearingDetailsForm.astro';
import SubscriptionCard from './SubscriptionCard.astro';
import PlanPills from './PlanPills.astro';

interface Props {
  /** Empty means nothing is gating the ACCOUNT. This screen can still render, because a second shop
   *  of a paying seller has a hold of its own that no account-level check can see. */
  holds: PublishHold[];
  /** The shop this screen is about — the one the dashboard is currently on. */
  store: { id: string; name: string; tier?: string; publishedAt?: string };
  /** Whether THIS shop's fee is in the standing order. A paying seller who opened a second shop has
   *  cleared every account-level hold and still owes for this one (`lib/store-plan.ts`). */
  storeInPlan: boolean;
  /** A card is on file and PayMe have not been asked for anything yet — the state the whole clearing
   *  review is spent in since 2026-08-24 (`lib/subscription-arm.ts`). */
  cardOnFile: boolean;
  /** Whose card it is — handed down to the card fields, never asked of the seller again. */
  payer: { name: string; email: string; phone: string };
  kyc: Partial<MerchantKyc>;
  derivedCategory: string | null;
  kycComplete: boolean;
  /** What PayMe actually hold. The only honest source for step 3, because the holds say what is
   *  BLOCKING and cannot distinguish an approval that is finished from one nobody asked for. */
  clearing: { state: 'ready' | 'missing-details' | 'not-opened' | 'awaiting-approval' } | null;
  planName: string;
  /** Every plan's display name, for the picker. Lives in the pricing copy rather than the
   *  dashboard's, so it is handed in rather than looked up — no component below this one reaches
   *  into another screen's translations. */
  planNames: Record<SellerTierId, string>;
  subscription: { status: number | null; nextCharge?: string; endsAt?: string; priceAgorot?: number; storeFees?: { storeId: string; storeName: string; tier: string; feeAgorot: number }[]; cardSavedAt?: string; providerRef?: string } | null;
  subscriptionPaying: boolean;
  /** PayMe's own onboarding page for this seller, when there is one. */
  signupLink?: string;
  d: ReturnType<typeof getT>['dashboard'];
}
const {
  holds, store, storeInPlan, cardOnFile, payer, kyc, derivedCategory, kycComplete, clearing, planName, planNames,
  subscription, subscriptionPaying, signupLink, d,
} = Astro.props;

/**
 * ── Step 1 is finished when HIS part is (2026-08-25) ──
 *
 * It used to key off the `clearing-details` hold, i.e. off PayMe having an account — and since the
 * account is no longer opened when the form is submitted (`subscription-arm.ts`: it costs ₪65 a
 * month for ever and cannot be closed, so it waits until he commits), that hold now lifts AFTER
 * step 2. Keyed off it, step 1 would sit open while the step below it was being worked on.
 *
 * `kycComplete` is the honest test: everything PayMe require has been given. What happens with it
 * afterwards is steps 2 and 3.
 */
const detailsDone = kycComplete || !holds.includes('clearing-details');
/**
 * ── This was WRONG for a day and the owner caught it (2026-08-25) ──
 *
 * *"עכשיו יש וי ירוק גם ליד אישור חברת הסליקה, זה בכוונה? נראה שלא, כי הכל אצלי ירוק עכשיו אבל
 * החנות עדיין לא באוויר."* It was not.
 *
 * It read `detailsDone && !holds.includes('clearing-approval')`, which was sound while `detailsDone`
 * meant *the account exists*: no account ⇒ no approval hold ⇒ but `detailsDone` false, so the `&&`
 * held the line. Moving step 1 onto `kycComplete` took that prop away — a seller who had typed the
 * form and no card had `detailsDone` true, no account, therefore no `clearing-approval` hold, and
 * this went green for a review that had not been asked for. Every step ticked, shop dark, and the
 * overview still asking for details. Two edits, each defensible, and the bug was in the gap.
 *
 * The lesson is in the shape, not the values: **an absent hold is not an achievement.** Holds say
 * what is blocking, so "not blocked" covers both finished and not-yet-started, and a green tick is
 * a claim about the first. Derived from the account's own state instead — `clearing.state` is
 * `'ready'` only when PayMe hold an approved business.
 */
const approvalDone = clearing?.state === 'ready';
/**
 * **This shop's** step, not the account's: a plan chosen and a card on file, or already being
 * billed for this shop. A seller can be paying and still owe for the shop he is looking at.
 *
 * It is done when the card is SAVED, not when it is charged — the charge happens by itself when
 * approval lands (`lib/subscription-arm.ts`), so a seller who has done everything asked of him must
 * see a finished step rather than an open one he cannot close.
 */
const cardDone = cardOnFile || (!!store.publishedAt && storeInPlan) || (subscriptionPaying && storeInPlan);

/**
 * Is the shop actually on the site? **The screen is about that, not about the steps** — and keying
 * it off the steps alone left a real hole: a seller who had finished all three sat in front of a
 * dashboard showing nothing at all, because `current` was null while his shop was still down. That
 * window is up to half an hour wide in production (the sweep's interval) and it is exactly the
 * moment he is most likely to be looking. Found by driving it, 2026-08-24.
 */
const liveDone = !!store.publishedAt;

/** The first step that is not done — the only one that opens. `approval` can be current without
 *  being actionable, which is the point of it being a step at all. **Null with the shop still down
 *  is a real state**: everything is done and the publication sweep has not run yet. */
const current: 'details' | 'card' | 'approval' | null =
  !detailsDone ? 'details'
  : !cardDone ? 'card'
  : !approvalDone ? 'approval'
  : null;

const STEPS = [
  { id: 'details', title: d.glStepDetails, done: detailsDone, note: d.glStepDetailsNote },
  { id: 'card', title: d.glStepSubscription, done: cardDone, note: d.glStepSubscriptionNote },
  {
    id: 'approval',
    title: d.glStepApproval,
    done: approvalDone,
    /**
     * **The review has not started until the details are sent**, and the note used to say it had
     * either way (owner, 2026-08-25: *"נראה לי מוזר שזה לא משתנה במידה ופרטי העסק לא מולאו"*).
     * Since every unfinished step opens now, this one is on screen from the very first minute — so
     * a seller who had typed nothing was told a seven-day clock was already running for him.
     */
    /**
     * Three states, because there are three (2026-08-25). The review has not been ASKED for until
     * the details reach PayMe, and they reach PayMe when the card is saved — so a seller who has
     * finished the form has neither "nothing typed yet" nor "a clock is running".
     *
     * Read from the account, never from `detailsDone`: that is the reading that put a green tick on
     * this step for a review nobody had requested.
     */
    note: clearing?.state === 'awaiting-approval' || clearing?.state === 'ready' ? d.glStepApprovalNote
      : clearing?.state === 'not-opened' ? d.glStepApprovalAfterCard
      : d.glStepApprovalWaiting,
  },
] as const;

/**
 * Which step he is ON — the POSITION of the open one, not a count of finished ones.
 *
 * Those are not the same number, and the difference is visible: the steps run in order but they can
 * finish out of order. A seller whose first shop is already live and who has just opened a second
 * has the LAST step done and the first outstanding, and counting ticks reported "step 2 of 3" over
 * a screen whose only open step was the first. Caught by driving the real dashboard, 2026-08-24 —
 * reasoning about it had produced the count.
 */
const stepNumber = current ? STEPS.findIndex((s) => s.id === current) + 1 : STEPS.length;

/** The one sentence at the top, and it answers the question the owner asked for: can this shop go
 *  on the site or not. Each state names WHO is holding it, because "not live" alone is what sends a
 *  seller looking through four boxes for the reason. */
const stateLine =
  current === 'details' ? d.glStateDetails
  : current === 'card' ? d.glStateLive
  // Two different waits, and the difference is the whole point of the change of 2026-08-24: a
  // seller with a card on file is finished and waiting, and the sentence has to say so rather than
  // leaving him reading the same "we are reviewing you" as somebody who has not decided yet.
  : current === 'approval' ? (cardOnFile ? d.glStateApprovalArmed : d.glStateApproval)
  // Nothing left to do and the shop not up yet — the minutes between the last hold lifting and the
  // sweep running. Saying "your shop is live" here would be the one lie this screen can tell.
  : d.glStateGoingUp;

const activeTier = (SELLER_TIERS.find((t) => t.id === store.tier)?.id ?? null) as SellerTierId | null;
---

{!liveDone && (
  <section class="card card--flat p-4 md:p-5" id="go-live" data-holds={holds.join(',')} data-store={store.id}>

    {/* ── The answer, before any of the detail ──────────────────────────────────────────────
        State, position, picture. Nothing here is clickable and nothing here is a step: it is the
        summary a seller reads in one second and then either acts on or closes the tab reassured. */}
    <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3 flex-wrap">
      <h3 class="text-[1.05rem] font-bold m-0">{d.glTitle.replace('{store}', store.name)}</h3>
      <p class="muted m-0 text-[0.8rem] tabular-nums">{d.glStepOf.replace('{n}', String(stepNumber)).replace('{total}', String(STEPS.length))}</p>
    </div>
    <p class="m-0 mt-1 text-[0.88rem] font-semibold">{stateLine}</p>

    {/**
      * ── The progress indicator is the LIST, not a bar above it (owner, 2026-08-25) ──
      *
      * *"המד שלבים הזה, הוא לא מוצא חן בעיניי, אולי הוא צריך להיות אנכי לשבת ליד השלבים עצמם? אני
      * גם רוצה שהוא יהפוך לירוק כשחלק אחד בו הושלם."*
      *
      * The bar was three segments floating above three rows that said the same thing — the reader
      * had to map one onto the other, and the mapping was the only information in it. A vertical
      * rail cannot be misread that way: each marker sits on its own row, and the line between two
      * markers is the progress between those two steps. It is the shape a checkout or an order
      * tracker has settled on for the same reason.
      *
      * **Green means done, and only done.** The connector above a completed marker is green
      * because that stretch is behind him; the one below the open step is not, because it is not.
      *
      * Built from the row's own layout rather than an absolute overlay: a marker column and a
      * content column, with the connector drawn as the marker cell's background. Nothing is
      * positioned against a measured height, so a step whose body grows — the ten-field clearing
      * form, four plan pills, three card iframes — cannot tear the line.
      */}
    <ol class="list-none p-0 m-0 mt-4 flex flex-col">
      {STEPS.map((step, i) => (
        <li
          class="grid grid-cols-[1.6rem_1fr] gap-x-2"
          data-step={step.id}
          data-state={step.done ? 'done' : step.id === current ? 'current' : 'waiting'}
        >
          {/* The marker column. `row-span-2` so the connector runs the full height of the step —
              title AND body — which is what makes the line continuous down a long form. */}
          <div class="row-span-2 flex flex-col items-center">
            {/* The stub above the first marker is suppressed: a line arriving from nowhere reads
                as a step that was cut off. */}
            <span
              class:list={[
                'w-[2px] h-2 shrink-0',
                i === 0 ? 'opacity-0' : step.done || STEPS[i - 1]!.done ? '[background:var(--color-success)]' : '[background:var(--color-border)]',
              ]}
              aria-hidden="true"
            ></span>
            {step.done ? (
              <span class="shrink-0 w-[1.35rem] h-[1.35rem] rounded-full grid place-items-center [background:var(--color-success)] text-white" aria-hidden="true">
                <svg width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg>
              </span>
            ) : (
              <span
                class:list={[
                  'shrink-0 w-[1.35rem] h-[1.35rem] rounded-full grid place-items-center text-[0.72rem] font-bold tabular-nums border',
                  step.id === current
                    ? '[border-color:var(--color-accent)] [color:var(--color-accent)] [background:color-mix(in_srgb,var(--color-accent)_10%,transparent)]'
                    : '[border-color:var(--color-border)] [color:var(--color-muted)]',
                ]}
                aria-hidden="true"
              >{i + 1}</span>
            )}
            {/* The rest of the rail, filling whatever this step's body turns out to be. Green only
                when this step is behind him. */}
            <span
              class:list={[
                'w-[2px] flex-1 min-h-2',
                i === STEPS.length - 1 ? 'opacity-0' : step.done ? '[background:var(--color-success)]' : '[background:var(--color-border)]',
              ]}
              aria-hidden="true"
            ></span>
          </div>

          <div class="min-w-0 pb-5">
            <p class={`m-0 text-[0.92rem] font-semibold leading-[1.35rem] ${step.done || step.id !== current ? '[color:var(--color-muted)]' : ''}`}>{step.title}</p>

          {/* Every step that is not finished explains itself. A done one is its marker and nothing
              else. */}
          {!step.done && (
            <p class="muted m-0 mt-1 text-[0.84rem]">{step.note}</p>
          )}

          {/**
            * ── Rendered while the details are still HIS, not only while they are unfinished ──
            *
            * `!detailsDone` meant the block vanished the instant the last field was typed, taking
            * the summary and the edit button with it: the owner finished the form, the step went
            * green, and there was no longer anything on the page to press (*"אי אפשר בעצם לערוך
            * אותו, למה?"*). The step being done is not a reason to hide what was done — every other
            * completed step on this screen keeps its content.
            *
            * The window that matters is the one between saving the form and PayMe receiving it,
            * which since 2026-08-25 is a real span of time rather than an instant. Inside it a typo
            * in a ח״פ is still fixable; outside it nothing here reaches them and the form says so
            * instead of offering an edit it cannot deliver.
            */}
          {step.id === 'details' && clearing?.state !== 'ready' && (
            <div class="mt-3">
              {/* `sent` means **PayMe hold these details**, and it must not be `detailsDone`
                  (owner, 2026-08-25: *"עכשיו יש וי ליד פרטי העסק והוא ירוק, ואי אפשר בעצם לערוך
                  אותו, למה?"*). Step 1 completing is about HIM — everything asked for is typed —
                  while this decides whether the form is still his to change. They were the same
                  fact until the account moved to card-save; now there is a real window between
                  them, and it is exactly the window in which a typo is still fixable. Locking the
                  form there took away the last chance to correct a ח״פ before it went to a
                  processor who will not amend it. */}
              {/* `'ready'` cannot occur here — the block above only renders while the state is NOT
                  ready — so `awaiting-approval` is the whole of "a processor holds these". */}
              <ClearingDetailsForm kyc={kyc} derivedCategory={derivedCategory} complete={kycComplete} sent={clearing?.state === 'awaiting-approval'} bare d={d} />
            </div>
          )}

          {/* PayMe's own page, and the only thing there is to offer on a step nobody can act on:
              a seller they have questions about finishes his details there rather than here. */}
          {step.id === 'approval' && !approvalDone && signupLink && (
            <a class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm mt-3" href={signupLink} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
              {d.payClearingLink}
            </a>
          )}

          {/**
            * ── OPEN whenever it is unfinished, never "only when it is your turn" ──
            *
            * Owner, 2026-08-25: *"אחרי שאני שולח פרטים לחברת הסליקה, אי אפשר להמשיך למילוי מסלול
            * וכרטיס?!"*. The screen opened exactly one step, so a seller who had finished the first
            * and was waiting on PayMe could not do the second — and the second depends on PayMe for
            * nothing at all: the plan is ours and the card is charged to OUR merchant. The wait and
            * the commitment are independent, so making one wait for the other was ordering for its
            * own sake, and it cost the exact thing the card step exists to buy.
            */}
          {/**
            * ── A finished step keeps what it is about (owner, 2026-08-25) ──
            *
            * *"נראה שהמוכר לא רואה איזה מסלול הוא בחר, הוא רק רואה שנבחר מסלול."* And he was right:
            * the whole block disappeared on `cardDone`, so the moment he chose, the plan's NAME,
            * its price and its commission all left the screen — replaced by a tick meaning "a plan
            * was picked", which is the one thing he already knew.
            *
            * The same fault as step 1's vanishing summary, and the same fix: state what was done.
            * The plan is also still changeable here — `/api/seller/tier` moves it from the next
            * charge, and while nothing has been charged at all there is not even a next charge to
            * wait for — so the chooser stays rather than becoming something he must go and find.
            */}
          {step.id === 'card' && cardDone && (
            <div class="mt-3">
              <dl class="grid grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-[0.86rem] m-0">
                <dt class="muted">{d.glPlanTitle}</dt>
                <dd class="m-0 font-semibold justify-self-start">
                  {d.subPlanValue.replace('{name}', planName).replace('{fee}', String(SELLER_TIERS.find((t) => t.id === (activeTier ?? SELLER_TIERS[0]!.id))!.monthlyFee)).replace('{per}', d.subPerMonth)}
                </dd>
                <dt class="muted">{d.subCommission}</dt>
                <dd class="m-0 font-semibold justify-self-start" id="sub-commission">{SELLER_TIERS.find((t) => t.id === (activeTier ?? SELLER_TIERS[0]!.id))!.commissionPercent}%</dd>
              </dl>
              <div class="mt-3">
                <PlanPills storeId={store.id} tier={store.tier} planNames={planNames} d={d} />
              </div>
            </div>
          )}

          {step.id === 'card' && !cardDone && (
            <div class="mt-3">
              {/* The plan chooser used to be drawn here and is now inside the card below, which is
                  the only component that renders it in BOTH of a shop's lives — being taken live,
                  and live (`PlanPills.astro`). Nothing moved on screen: in `bare` the card draws no
                  frame and the pills are still its first row. */}
              <div class="mt-4">
                <SubscriptionCard
                  storeId={store.id}
                  planName={planName}
                  tier={store.tier as SellerTierId | undefined}
                  subscription={subscription}
                  planNames={planNames}
                  paying={subscriptionPaying}
                  storeInPlan={storeInPlan}
                  cardOnFile={cardOnFile}
                  payer={payer}
                  bare
                  d={d}
                />
              </div>
            </div>
          )}
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <p class="muted m-0 mt-4 text-[0.8rem]">{d.glAutomatic}</p>
  </section>
)}
